# 3.2 MB of raw OSM tracks over the region's bbox, two thirds of which the build
# drops. Same treatment as the hydrology draft next to it: derived, reproducible
# with tools/fetch-roads.mjs, and only the built roads.json is worth history.
tools/roads-draft.json
tools/landcover-osm-draft.json
tools/landcover-unshipped/
tools/ndvi-draft.bin
tools/ndvi-draft.json

# The native DTM mosaic: a build input, not a temp file, and deliberately outside
# the history (user's decision, 2026-08-14). 145.9 MB at 5 m/px, for a file the
# viewer never downloads and a static host has no use for; git would keep every
# version of it forever. Its master GeoTIFF lives in ~/pngp-dtm-work/merged/, and
# tools/dtm-source/README.md says how to reproduce both. The sidecar stays tracked -
# it is 3 KB and it carries the calibration and the CC BY attributions.
DEM/pngp_heightmap.png
DEM/*.tif

# The binary heightfields the viewer downloads: the base grid and the two levels
# of the optional high-resolution tier (user's decision, 2026-08-17 - "rimuovendo
# da git i file bin pesanti, anche dallo storico"). Every .bin ever committed came
# to 136.6 MB of blobs, a third of it superseded by the next rebuild, and each
# rebuild added ~48 MB more to a history that can never give it back.
#
# They are DERIVED, not authored: tools/process-heightmap.mjs makes the base and
# tools/build-height-tier.mjs the tier levels, both deterministically, so the same
# mosaic yields byte-identical output and the hashed filenames still match their
# manifests. tools/README.md says which mosaic each one needs - and they are not
# the same mosaic, which is the trap.
#
# A pattern, not three names: the filenames carry a content hash, so every rebuild
# invents a new one and a list would go stale on the next run.
public/data/*.bin

# The orthophoto sheets, as they come from the region. One 1:5.000 element of the
# Valle d'Aosta Ortofoto 2024 is ~400 MB of zipped 20 cm TIF - a build INPUT, on the
# same footing as the DTM mosaic above: derived output ships, the source does not.
# tools/dev/probe-orthophoto.mjs documents where each sheet comes from, and the two
# public endpoints that turn a coordinate into a download.
tools/ortho-source/

# The orthophoto SHEETS the viewer downloads. Derived, reproducible from the sources and the
# one gdal_translate line in ortho.json's generatedBy. This started as a way to keep TRIALS
# out of a history that can never give the megabytes back - resolution and extent were what
# was being decided, and that lesson was paid for once already with the .bin files above.
#
# THAT IS NO LONGER WHY IT IS HERE. The mosaic was settled and shipped on 2026-08-21 - 129
# sheets at 2 m/px, 22.16 MB - and the user's decision that day was to KEEP it ignored anyway:
# the deployed gh-pages branch carries all 129 sheets, so a copy exists off this machine
# without putting permanent weight in a history that can never give it back. Rebuilding from
# scratch is 38 GB of the Region's bandwidth and about two hours, which is the cost of being
# wrong about that.
#
# The MANIFEST stays tracked either way: 56 kB for 129 sheets, and it carries the licence,
# the attribution and the grid the atlas places itself from.
# A pattern loose enough to survive a rename, which the first version was not: it read
# `ortho.*.webp` and the levels are named `ortho-05.<hash>.webp`, so renaming them silently
# un-ignored all three. Caught by looking, one commit before it mattered.
public/data/ortho*.webp
public/data/ortho/*.webp
# (The build's "already done" markers used to need a line here too. They now live in
# tools/ortho-source/.stamps/, because everything under public/ is copied into dist/ and
# published - a full run would have put 129 build markers on the website.)
